<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Android Network Security Config for rustdar.

  Referenced by android:networkSecurityConfig on <application> in
  AndroidManifest.xml, and compiled into resources.arsc by aapt2 as part of the
  Gradle build.

  This file is why that Gradle build exists. cargo-apk, which built the APK
  before, could not deliver it:

    * Its AndroidManifest generator has a fixed attribute set on <application>
      (debuggable, theme, extractNativeLibs, usesCleartextTraffic, label, icon,
      hasCode, meta-data). `networkSecurityConfig` is not in it, and the metadata
      table in Cargo.toml has no passthrough for arbitrary attributes.
    * The attribute's value is a *resource reference*, so the file has to be
      compiled by aapt2 into `resources.arsc` and given a resource ID. The
      `classes.dex` injection that build-android.sh used to do worked only
      because a DEX at the APK root needs no resource table entry; an `res/xml/`
      file does. Adding it to the ZIP would have achieved nothing.

  ===========================================================================
  Why the base config permits cleartext while every rustdar domain forbids it

  rustls-platform-verifier issue #221: on Android, certificates whose revocation
  data is published over plain HTTP fail to verify when the app forbids cleartext
  traffic. Android's TrustManager fetches the CRL itself, and that fetch is
  subject to the app's own network security policy; when it is blocked the
  verifier cannot prove the certificate is unrevoked and rejects it. The cause is
  the blocked fetch, not a hard-fail revocation policy.

  api.weather.gov is exactly that shape. Verified against the live endpoint:

      issuer  = C=US, O=Let's Encrypt, CN=R13
      AIA     = CA Issuers - URI:http://r13.i.lencr.org/   (no OCSP URI at all)
      CRL DP  = URI:http://r13.c.lencr.org/32.crl          (cleartext)

  With no OCSP responder to fall back on, that cleartext CRL is the only
  revocation source, so blocking cleartext app-wide breaks HTTPS to weather.gov.

  Hence: `cleartextTrafficPermitted="true"` in base-config, so the platform's own
  revocation fetches succeed, and `false` per-domain for every host rustdar
  actually talks to, so none of rustdar's own traffic can travel in the clear.
  Application code is not trusted to get this right on its own (every client is
  additionally built with `.https_only(true)`, see rustdar_radar::tls::client),
  but this file is what makes it the OS's decision rather than ours.

  NB: an XML comment may not contain a double hyphen, and aapt2 parses this file
  for real. Keep prose here free of them or the resource build fails.

  includeSubdomains is set on every entry, including the ones whose host is
  fixed. This file used to say it was "omitted where the exact host is fixed",
  but no entry had ever actually omitted it, and the first one that did failed
  the build: Android Lint raises NetworkSecurityConfig / "Missing
  includeSubdomains attribute" as a fatal error under lintVitalRelease.

  Lint is right, and specifically right about this file. Denying cleartext for
  a bare host leaves its subdomains falling through to base-config, which here
  permits cleartext on purpose. So the safe default is the wide one, and the
  test described below asserts the attribute is present on every entry so a
  release build cannot be broken by this again.

  ===========================================================================
  Keeping this list honest

  The claim above only holds while this list matches what the application
  actually fetches, and a hand-maintained copy of another crate's constants does
  not stay matched on its own. It had already drifted in both directions:
  aviationweather.gov was still listed after METAR moved off it, and its
  replacement (Iowa Environmental Mesonet) was never added, so the one live
  origin missing from this file was silently falling through to base-config,
  where cleartext is permitted.

  `rustdar-platform/src/network_security_config.rs` parses this file at test
  time and asserts, both ways, that it covers exactly the origins
  `rustdar_radar::sources::DataSources::production()` and the tile sources
  declare. Adding an origin there and not here now fails `cargo test`.

  Generating the file outright was considered and rejected: the attribute on
  <application> is a resource reference, so the XML has to exist on disk for
  aapt2 to compile into resources.arsc, and a generated one could not carry any
  of the reasoning above. A test gives the same guarantee against divergence
  and leaves the prose where a reader will find it.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <!-- Permits the platform's own cleartext CRL/AIA fetches. Does NOT permit
         rustdar's traffic: every domain below overrides it back to false. -->
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <!-- System store only, and explicitly no bundled roots: trust must
                 stay revocable by the OS and updatable without a new binary.
                 `user` is deliberately absent from the *default*; add it in a
                 debug-only overlay if you need to MITM for debugging. -->
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>

    <!-- NWS / NOAA: active alerts and zone geometry from api.weather.gov, and
         SPC outlooks, mesoscale discussions and storm reports from
         www.spc.noaa.gov. Both DataSources::nws_api_base and
         DataSources::spc_base land here. -->
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">weather.gov</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">noaa.gov</domain>
    </domain-config>

    <!-- Iowa Environmental Mesonet: current ASOS/METAR observations
         (DataSources::iem_base). This is where METAR comes from now; it used to
         be aviationweather.gov, which was still listed here long after nothing
         fetched from it while this host was not listed at all. -->
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">mesonet.agron.iastate.edu</domain>
    </domain-config>

    <!-- Open-Meteo forecast API: environmental sounding heights (0C and -20C
         levels) per radar site, for the hail products
         (DataSources::sounding_base). -->
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">api.open-meteo.com</domain>
    </domain-config>

    <!-- Everything served out of S3: the NEXRAD Level II archive and realtime
         chunks, Level III products (which used to come over TGFTP and now come
         from the unidata-nexrad-level3 bucket), HRRR GRIB2, and GOES granules
         for GLM lightning. All of them are
         https://BUCKET.s3.amazonaws.com/KEY, so one entry with
         includeSubdomains covers every bucket. -->
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">s3.amazonaws.com</domain>
    </domain-config>

    <!-- Basemap and label tiles (walkers to CartoDB via Fastly), plus the
         attribution link the map footer opens. -->
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">global.ssl.fastly.net</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">openstreetmap.org</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
